But {A} and {B} also measure {D}, therefore {A}, {B}, and {C} measure {D}.

I say next that it is also the least that they measure.

If not, {A}, {B}, and {C} measure some number {E} less than {D}.

Since {A}, {B}, and {C} measure {E}, therefore {A} and {B} measure {E}. Therefore the least number measured by {A} and {B} also measures {E}. [!just VII.35]

But {D} is the least number measured by {A} and {B}, therefore {D} measures {E}, the greater the less, which is impossible.

Therefore {A}, {B}, and {C} do not measure any number less than {D}. Therefore {D} is the least that {A}, {B}, and {C} measure.

Next, let {C:canvas_1} not measure {D:canvas_1}.

Take {E:canvas_1}, the least number measured by {C:canvas_1} and {D:canvas_1}. [!just VII.34]

Since {A:canvas_1} and {B:canvas_1} measure {D:canvas_1}, and {D:canvas_1} measures {E:canvas_1}, therefore {A:canvas_1} and {B:canvas_1} also measure {E:canvas_1}. But {C:canvas_1} also measures {E:canvas_1}, therefore {A:canvas_1}, {B:canvas_1}, and {C:canvas_1} also measure {E:canvas_1}.

